    summary: 'A large, intense area of low pressure tracks in from the Atlantic, bringing gales or severe gales, heavy rain and large waves, usually followed by squally showers.',
    body: [
      'Deep lows form where warm and cold air meet beneath the jet stream. The Met Office notes that, in general, the lower the central pressure the stronger the winds. As the system passes, the warm front brings rain, the warm sector mild drizzly air, the cold front a narrow band of heavy rain and a wind change, and the cold air behind it gusty showers, with snow on northern hills in winter.',
      'Many deep lows are "fairly typical winter storms — notable but not exceptional", as the Met Office wrote of Storm Erik (2019). Their impact depends heavily on track, timing and season: trees in full leaf in early autumn or late spring are more vulnerable, and a storm arriving with high spring tides can bring coastal flooding.',
    ],
    regions: [['west-coast', 'high', 'Exposed coasts and headlands record the strongest gusts.'], ['north-west', 'high', 'Most often closest to the low centres.'], ['south-coast', 'moderate', 'Storms tracking further south bring severe gusts to the south coast (Needles Old Battery often records the highest).']],
